and then the second part is um i still  do love some philosophy  and i think the education school gives  me really helpful  theories and frameworks to which to  understand  um social processes and then the  business school and the design school  have like  lots of applied learning classes where  you can quickly learn like  the skills um which i could have easily  ended up in just a master's in education  program and i would have so  missed out on all of the like applied  learning  that the business school really excels  at  yeah i definitely echo a lot of  isabelle's um  sentiments because i actually think that  doing both at the same time has  bolstered  my experience um because i didn't i  didn't i never really thought that like  my mba experience in and of itself was  just going to be sort of applied to any  sort of business i was always sort of  more impact oriented but i didn't have a  ton of  exposure to the education world um  professionally before  coming to the gsb um so i would say two  things one is that to issa's point  like the community both sort of at in  the ed school but i would actually say  that the joint degree students has been  such a  such a really nice group of friends that  we formed over time  and i think moments where i felt a  little bit like  what about when we don't maximize profit  or what are the other values that matter  in this certain case i can always look  to my peers in the joint  degree program and know that i have a  home of people who are also sitting in  class  nodding along with me and so a lot of  those cross um  sort of cross-listed classes have been  really really helpful  um isa mentioned the disruptions in  education with professor erstein was  was really formative for me um and then  i think the second piece is  in some ways being a part of the ed  school has given me  um you know just a break from the gsb  sometimes honestly like sometimes it's  just nice to be able to bounce back and  forth  and you know meet people who have very  very different aspirations and career  trajectories than we  than we will um and it also serves as a  reminder that  successful and fulfilling careers look  very different for very different people  um and it's just nice to come into  contact and become friends with these  people  especially you know when you have two  years of being immersed in one and  sometimes also get an outlet in the  other so i feel very lucky to  to have done the joint degree  awesome thank you so much for sharing  those perspectives  the next question comes from nicole i  love this question  nicole asks who is the most inspiring  person you've met at stanford  and why seems like a really hard  question  too but i'm very curious about the  answer myself  um i'm gonna make andrew blush  um but andrew organized  the most beautiful display of allyship  i've seen  um it was this rally and support of  black classmates and  peers and professors and members of our  community at the height of  exactly one year ago um the height of  the black lives matter movement  and there was poetry and there were  singing and reached out to the dean to  make sure that  um we could have an in-person gathering  at a time when it wasn't allowed  and it was safe and it was really  inspiring so shout out to andrew  that was beautiful thank you for that  i know it must be a very difficult  question to answer  we can always come back to it um but as  a  isabelle thank you so much for sharing  that i think that that's really really  beautiful  and um one thing we used to say in our  presentations  is that the world needs  needs principled leaders now more than  ever  and i think that that's something that  is a big part of the stanford gsb  ethos and culture um  so it's i think it's really good to hear  that reinforced through the the actual  experiences that you all have had  on campus and in our community so thank  you so much for sharing that  um you can always come back to this  question i have  i have one that comes to mind um  maybe not what you were envisioning but  um  the gsb residences housing team and  is absolutely incredible and especially  as we transition into a totally virtual  experience  watching um arthel and his team just  essentially make things happen overnight  for us and keep us safe and to keep us  healthy and protected and just go above  and beyond  um he wins the distinguished service  award i feel like every five seconds so  probably knows this already but  just like the support team behind how  the gsb operates has been  so inspiring and sometimes i i just like  look look around and i'm like the reason  this looks the way it does and i can  just go about my day without having to  worry about  food or housing or you know whatever is  happening in the world or is because  there is like an army of people behind  the scenes who work so hard for us  um so i wanted to give them a shout out  awesome thank you for that so the next  question  comes from zach who asks could you talk  about your global experiences  and how you've applied those learnings  in your mba experience  so for those of you in the audience who  don't know we have a  global experience requirement as part of  the  mba education because we want our  students to  to learn more about how business happens  around the world  and to expand their global business  perspective  um maybe i'll throw this question to sam  and andrew just because i know things  have been  probably a little bit different during  the past year and some change  so sam andrew any any insights on the  global experience that you'd be able to  share  yeah i can go ahead and um i'll keep it  brief but i'd say a few things one  is a piece of advice and one is  so i did two global experiences i did a  gmx  um which is where you do an internship  in another country and i did a gst which  is like  the um what most people do  during their mba where they do like a  study trip  that's run by the second year students  in 