eally if you end up going to just be a  real piece of advice i have and i really  wish i had been able to do  one of the global studies programs  before school started people really  loved those and came in with a very  strong group of friends  and i had a wedding so i couldn't do it  but it was um  something that i really feel like i i  wish i had been able to do so if you  have the opportunity to do that  um in addition to all the unofficial  events i think it really people  very much love those programs  yeah i i think just reflecting on my  experience um  so i grew up in a very uh  u.s americana type setting uh  in the philadelphia and suburbs  and i didn't leave the country until  after i had graduated from undergrad  but i did live abroad before going to  the gsb and it just  revolutionized so much about  the way i thought my values  how i defined success kind of all of  these factors that just made  having an international and  multicultural experience really  important for me and that was also a  contributing factor in kind of you know  business school and decisions made  while at the gsb i was just reflecting  now and realized  i did four global experiences  as well as um an internship  uh in brazil so in terms of  kind of the opportunities and  experiences afforded to me  you know i went to asia for the first  time  lived in europe for the first time i  traveled to and lived in brazil for the  first time  and accumulated professional experiences  in those settings  academic experiences in those settings  language culture and all of these other  aspects of being present that i just  wouldn't have necessarily had the  opportunity to pursue um if just one or  two factors were different  around you know my experience or or the  opportunities within the gsb  and so for those who who might be  outside of the us  and those who are interested in going  outside of the u.s perhaps after the  mba you know there's a lot of  possibility and opportunity  in what you can do to make that a  reality and  you know if you if that's what's  important to you  you know like some of the other things  we talked about you can really lean  in to um what's offered through the  program  um and you know i'm back abroad now and  i expect to stay abroad for a long time  um and in every place i go  right there's either a network or  perhaps an opportunity  or you know at least some type of  support that i think exists  and stems from the gsb community  awesome thank you so much so i know we  only have a couple minutes left i wish  i wish we could chat for much longer  you'll see that there's a poll  up please let us know how we're doing  we're here to serve you  so please let us know what we can do  better or what we're doing well  so i'll throw out two two more questions  it's very difficult because  our attendees have submitted some really  really amazing questions so i'm going to  throw out two  questions and you'll each have an  opportunity to respond  and you can pick which question you want  to respond to  so there have been a couple questions  about timing  a couple students have kind of been  wondering like how did you know that it  was the right time to get an mba if  or did you not know um so a question a  couple questions about timing  and then another question that i saw  asked if you could share your favorite  or most meaningful  class or experience that you've had so  those are the two questions you can pick  which one  you'd like to respond to and everybody  can can chime in  i'll just quickly say on timing you know  trust your your gut  there's no too early there's no too late  um  do it when it feels right um  and then if i can just speak to i think  two experiences that  i will carry with me um  i think for the rest of my life um we're  both  speaking um kind of larger scale  disclosure experiences and one was a low  keynote  um talking about diversity equity and  inclusion and my experience and  my identities and then the second 